# Custom modules

These are background extensions of existing Odoo apps. They never create a new app.

## Technical names

`int_<app>_customizations`

```
int_inventory_customizations
int_shippo_customizations
int_helpdesk_customizations
int_contacts_customizations
int_website_customizations
```

Do not use `int_base`, `rvsb_`, or an unprefixed name. Never set `application: True`.

A vendor integration that is not an extension of one Odoo app keeps the `int_`
prefix but drops the `_customizations` suffix, e.g. `int_victron_stock`. Do not
rename these to match the pattern above.

## Manifest

- `name`: `<App> Customizations` (no "Internal" in the display name)
- `author`: `Internal`
- `application`: `False`
- `version`: `19.0.x.y.z`
- `license`: `LGPL-3`
- `depends`: only the upstream Odoo apps being extended (e.g. `stock`, `helpdesk`)

## Layout

```
int_<app>_customizations/
  __init__.py
  __manifest__.py
  models/
  views/
  data/
```

One existing app per module. Do not dump Studio exports as `studio_customization`.

## Fields

Define fields in Python with clean names (`hts_code`, `weight_lbs`). Do not keep `x_studio_` prefixes.

If a field name changes after data exists, bump the module version and add
`migrations/<new_version>/pre-migrate.py` that renames the Postgres column (and
`ir.attachment.res_field` for binaries) before the ORM creates an empty field.

## Pushing

Odoo.sh builds every commit that lands on `main`, and a manifest that lists a
missing file takes the database down with an internal server error. So every
commit must stand on its own — never split a file move across two commits, and
never commit a partial staged subset of a refactor.

`tools/check_modules.py --rev HEAD` validates manifests, dependencies, init
hooks, Python syntax and XML for a commit. A `pre-push` hook runs it
automatically; enable it once per clone with:

```
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
```
